/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198481 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198481 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4228fb68-d9aa-54a5-8d69-9202b473f5c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198577 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198577 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198577 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1f/1f899ac64f4d16cc14255603642e9038ec0b76f6b3ec27da53d3692b25431444.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198577 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e90b093e-e3d7-5a59-aa98-97c5665b0473" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198577 #edb/ref 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{2},$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/61/616f6f5e952fa5634497b86ced1893c938cd76051ae0e0e0200bb323bff33238.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198578 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198578 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160418" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198578 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198578 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/41/413ff5566eedb8e99c8fb0f3d29b8effd0e146ee4367db93264a5392edd818c1.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198578 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2fca64ae-782f-5ae2-bf69-6525c7a1e239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198578 #edb/ref 1074 "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=2,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b2/b221864b2795f2b158e8c4d1f88da23437f8d043da6c31e0b5c8392ebc1118bd.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198580 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198580 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160481" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198580 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198580 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b8/b8874b14fa80881e69cb1daee180d2e342a27c5902e66fc75d43622278176b09.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198580 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9eb2357b-f9d2-552f-8ddb-db8ea02b5422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198580 #edb/ref 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcfc165548d8174ec0468dce53317fd0c8daa14565852c5e44d6764161723bed.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199267 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199267 #edb/ref 1061 42408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199267 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c75dcfe-d31b-552f-8b7f-bb0e2c83fbe1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199267 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199275 #edb/ref 1061 44184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199275 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8bab0285-041c-5645-bfb0-d1af1dccc616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199275 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199277 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199277 #edb/ref 1061 44217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199277 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e182722-189e-524f-a581-82e71805ddc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199277 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199278 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199278 #edb/ref 1061 44222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199278 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6da0258-e96c-5bbb-91d8-5c5aab571523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199278 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199279 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199279 #edb/ref 1061 44223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199279 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c28b3ebd-437b-5803-87ec-1e04e0f5c191" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199279 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199289 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199289 #edb/ref 1061 44308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199289 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "616ce947-0b5d-5274-955e-7ae934283b51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199289 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199308 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199308 #edb/ref 1061 44555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199308 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2349a1aa-a4dc-5683-a96c-be82f8b7ab2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199308 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199309 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199309 #edb/ref 1061 44556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199309 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21530539-0f1c-512e-92ce-ac7f86f8be41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199309 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199463 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5} \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nA sketch of $\\Delta$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n     \n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ onto the $xy$-plane, we need to transform each side (bottom, top, left, and right) of $\\Delta$ separately: \n\n![](images/06/062a3d1f13e8566770118650c92231ebe17f5eba2b4ae8d8b41722e33a72baff.png)\n\nIt can be shown that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$ So, let's proceed to find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by mapping these four sides." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199463 #edb/ref 1065 7615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199463 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "89fbd7db-7e0a-5047-9be9-1a886c8970f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199463 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199464 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the following transformation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v), \\qquad u > 0, \\quad 0\\leq v < 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nIf we substitute $u = r$ and $v = \\theta,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(r,\\theta) \\to (r \\cos \\theta, \\, r \\sin \\theta), \\qquad r > 0, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta < 2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that these are the usual formulas that we use to write the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta)$ of a point in the plane in Cartesian $(x,y)$ coordinates. \n\nThis gives us a fresh perspective of the meaning behind the usual $(r,\\theta)\\to(x,y)$ formulas:\n\n- Firstly, they describe a transformation that maps a region $\\Delta$ in the $(r,\\theta)$ plane (often a rectangle) to another region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Similarly, the inverse transformation (which always exists) maps a region $D$ in the $xy$-plane to a region $\\Delta$ in the $(r,\\theta)$ plane.\n\nThis idea becomes important later on. But, broadly speaking, many problems become easier after transforming them to another coordinate system where the domain under consideration is rectangular." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199464 #edb/ref 1065 7618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199464 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "49541498-bef0-5f43-a3fc-95ac9db70e7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199464 #edb/ref 1067 "Polar Coordinate Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199475 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n  \n\nOur goal is to find the image of the region $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's first find the images of the horizontal sides:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$ Substituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a line segment that lies on a line passing through the origin. The slope of this line must be\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{\\sin \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)}{\\cos \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)} = \\tan\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg),\n$$\n\nmeaning that the line makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. \nThe endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca644fa90870697e9cea1740c6432d43f682c650521217f19ea6f87c09cba0fd.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the top side, where $v=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$\n\n![](images/11/118c9d18e4d8158014643771c9fa018b1b15da38184f693b89d07f9fadd3c748.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199475 #edb/ref 1065 7670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199475 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8be5595b-5342-5f9e-9e69-9591c32d7780" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199475 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199476 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n  \n\nOur transformation $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the images of the vertical lines:\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e0e5a723b7a9fdc860bcf3f619b6db4bdbec24c06bc22f3121a620080969b6c.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1b9f163724e97d662192d5d274768b3e501843a2a2267adc5a3e1f5c6c9d230.png)\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/04/048606038e26fe79430899c284beff24921397879bb0ce5d46ab1f3653cab47e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199476 #edb/ref 1065 7671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199476 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "74b0f131-a207-5518-ade5-32ceacbf2263" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199476 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275652 #edb/ref 1080 "images/2e/2eb8b5f7ee3fe1b85960f1d6c87a2aabf58aae6f97394b2c7145cc98b39708dc.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275652 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275652 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1313dcb9-789f-52c3-a390-bf401c7faaaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275678 #edb/ref 1080 "The image is the empty set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275678 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275678 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15595742-55cf-5dc2-acd6-fdd7e56cd42b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275719 #edb/ref 1080 "images/b6/b611546ee3a8ae2714de1cc97dec62b9c750a39f858864b63d70be0d26a65b68.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275719 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275719 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "19ebaa46-ae1a-5015-968e-8dacf1cf46a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275753 #edb/ref 1080 "images/2b/2be15ae2d8bf7a309065ac33c97f5937d3fc8c12c5ca6ddc0c54ab44f555a5d1.png" #edb/ref 175921860